{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41464","model_call":20,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3088"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3088"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_595481664#4_1195118864","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3088","rank":3,"snippet":"and the causes that he served became more alive and powerful after his death than during the times when he was living. Plato and Aristotle have been held in high esteem because of their intellectual achievements and the fact that their ideas have been preserved through the writings that they produced. Socrates has also been recognized as an intellectual genius, but in addition, his career in the city of Athens has come to be regarded by many persons as an outstanding example of the <cover>virtues that he advocated. His humility, intellectual honesty, devotion to the public good, and loyalty to what he believed was morally right exemplify his conception of what constitutes the good life. </cover>Because of the quality of his living, along with the abiding truth of what he taught, the story of his trial and death is something that will continue to stir the imagination of people and to win for him their admiration and respect. With reference to the trial and death of Socrates, there are four dialogs that are especially relevant. They are the Euthyphro, the Apology, the Crito, and the Phaedo. In the Euthyphro, an attempt is made to answer the question \"What is piety?\" "},"time":16},"model_calls":20,"document_judgments":18,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
