{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-41464","model_call":24,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3092"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3092"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_20_1541559733#4_3367593754","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3092","rank":2,"snippet":"Three tips for cultivating humility 1. Embrace your humanness 2. Practice mindfulness and self-compassion 3. Express gratitude About the Author Vicki Zakrzewski We believe happiness should be free. \" , \"segment\": \"we become less likely to act aggressively, manipulate others, express dishonesty, and destroy resources. Instead, we take responsibility for and correct our mistakes, listen to others’ ideas, and keep our abilities in humble perspective. Who wouldn’t want that kind of leadership for our country—and the world? But the benefits of <cover>humility do not extend to just our leaders. Nascent research suggests that this lovely quality is good for us individually and for our relationships. </cover>For example, humble people handle stress more effectively and report higher levels of physical and mental well-being. They also show greater generosity, helpfulness, and gratitude —all things that can only serve to draw us closer to others. Three tips for cultivating humility Given what scientists have discovered about humility, it’s evident that cultivating this quality is not for the faint-hearted, nor does it appear overnight. Yet it would seem that one of the great rewards of humility is an inner freedom from having to protect ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_458419413#21_921426618","rank":1,"relevance":1}]},"time":6},"model_calls":24,"document_judgments":22,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
